  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A warming trend peaks Friday, with a cold air wedge building\nin for the weekend behind a cold front passage.\n\n \nUpper level ridging is expected to build over the southeast \nstates, with the northern periphery likely across the Ohio \nvalley and Mid- Atlantic states region. This will allow for \nincreasing temperatures through Friday before the arrival of a \ncold front pushes into the region late on Friday. \n\nBehind this cold front, surface high pressure is expected to \nslide from the northern Great lakes region into the Northeast, \nwhich will briefly lead to cold air wedging developing over \nVA/NC Saturday and Sunday. This will dramatically cool \ntemperatures areawide back to normal or just below normal for \nthe weekend. The possibility of a secondary shortwave trough and\nstronger cold front look to push into the region late Monday \ninto Tuesday, which looks to reinforce the cooler air into\nmidweek.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The current period of dry and clear weather ends late Friday\ninto the weekend with increasing chances for rain.\n\nWith the approach of a cold front on Friday night, and moisture\nfrom the remnants from Polo overriding the front, the region\nlooks to be in store for a couple days of cool and rainy\nconditions through the weekend. Rainfall totals in the 0.5-1.0\ninch range is anticipated with this system. Ensemble model \nguidance has shifted towards the surface high pressure and dry \nair aloft lifting slightly further north than yesterday's model\nsolutions. This will increase rainfall production across the \narea every so slightly; however, rainfall looks to remain light\nto moderate through the weekend overall. The cutoff line of dry\nvs moist air is quite evident and sharp at the 850mb level, and\nthe placement of this line as the frontal boundary stalls will \nplay a great role in how much precipitation the area sees over \nthe weekend. If this line remains across northern VA as is\ncurrently forecasted, much of the area will see beneficial \nrainfall; however, if this line of drier air penetrates further \nsouth beyond the I-64 corridor, then much of the area will miss \nout on any heavy rainfall.\n\nWe dry out next week with the introduction of another dry\nairmass behind the second reinforcing cold front.",
